<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philanthropinum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Philanthropinum was a progressive school founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> in 1774 by the educator Johann Bernhard Basedow with the support of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-frederick-franz">Leopold III Friedrich Franz</a> of Anhalt-Dessau. Inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideas, it taught through play, practical work, physical exercise and modern languages rather than rote learning. It closed in 1793, but it influenced many later schools and educational reformers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philanthropinum.jpg" alt="Philanthropinum" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M_H.DE · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dessau,_Palais_Dietrich,_Wissenschaftliche_Bibliothek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The school took its name from the Greek words for friend and human and followed the principles of philanthropinism, an educational movement of the German-speaking Enlightenment. It opened on 27 December 1774, founded by Johann Bernhard Basedow and Christian Heinrich Wolke; Basedow drew on the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a> on childhood and education. It began with one teacher and three pupils, among them Prince Frederick of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, but grew quickly as its reputation spread. Prince Franz supported it with money and with the Palais Dietrich, parts of which it used from 1777 and the whole from 1780.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Basedow stepped down in 1776. His successor, Joachim Heinrich Campe, left in 1777 after conflict with the younger teachers and founded a similar school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, and a board of directors then ran the institution. Its teachers included Ernst Christian Trapp, the first professor of education, Christian Gotthilf Salzmann, August Friedrich Wilhelm Crome, the artist Carl Wilhelm Kolbe, the poet Friedrich von Matthisson and the artist Friedrich Rehberg. At its peak in 1782-83 it had 53 pupils, some from western, northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Immanuel Kant supported it, calling for a quick revolution in education rather than slow reform.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Pupils were admitted regardless of sex, religion or state, though class still shaped their schooling: children of richer families spent more time on academic subjects and poorer ones did more manual work. All learnt handicrafts and took part in games and exercise, and the uniform was designed to be practical. Over 60 similar schools were founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, among them Carl Gottfried Neuendorf's Hauptschule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> of 1786 and Salzmann's school at Schnepfenthal, and others in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philanthropinum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His grandson Leopold III Friedrich Franz was a model Enlightenment ruler who laid out the landscape gardens of Wörlitz, built schools and founded the <b>Philanthropinum</b>, a progressive school, in 1774.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-dessau">Principality of Anhalt-Dessau</a></li></ul></section>
